# ShellCheck configuration for the whole repo. Tracked on purpose: it must apply
# to a bare `shellcheck scripts/foo.sh` typed by hand and to an editor's inline
# ShellCheck just as it does to `make lint-shell`. A gate whose verdict disagrees
# with the tool a developer runs by hand trains people to distrust the gate.
#
# Both settings exist to make ShellCheck resolve `source` the way the scripts
# actually resolve it at RUNTIME. Every script here sources its libs relative to
# its own location (`source "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib/workspace.sh"`, `source ./ports.sh`
# from within scripts/lib/), but ShellCheck's default is to resolve a relative
# source against the process CWD — so from the repo root it simply cannot find
# scripts/lib/ports.sh behind `source ./ports.sh`, and reports SC1091.
#
#   external-sources  — follow `source`d files at all (the -x flag). Without it
#                       ShellCheck refuses to read any file not named on the
#                       command line, and a `# shellcheck source=...` directive
#                       has no effect.
#   source-path       — SCRIPTDIR is the special value meaning "the directory of
#                       the script being checked", which is exactly the runtime
#                       semantics above.
#
# This is STRICTLY MORE analysis, not less: ShellCheck parses the sourced lib and
# sees the other side of every cross-file reference. That is also why it removes
# far more than the SC1091s — most of the "SC2034 appears unused" and "SC2329
# never invoked" reports in this repo were artifacts of ShellCheck not being able
# to see the consumer. Suppressing those would have papered over this
# misconfiguration and disarmed a check that is load-bearing for the genuine ones.
external-sources=true
source-path=SCRIPTDIR
